National elections in Cambodia are governed by the Law on the Election of the Members of National Assembly (LEMNA).
National elections are held every five years on a Sunday at a date determined by the prime minister.
One challenge that some Cambodian voters, such as factory workers, face is that their identification is for a location other than their current residence, usually based on their first home. This means that they must travel a long distance, first to register and then to vote.
At present, elections are only held within Cambodia’s territory, which means that even if otherwise eligible, Cambodian citizens cannot vote from abroad.
In order to register to vote, you are required to present yourself at the commune/sangkat office during the registration period. You must show documentation to prove that you are a Cambodian citizen, will be 18 years old or older on election day, and have residence in the commune/sangkat where you plan to vote.
