Licensed underCC BY-NC 2.0. A 1993 royal decree designated 23 protected areas covering about 3,273,300 ha, equal to around 18% of the country’s total land area, and brought them under the jurisdiction of the Ministry of Environment.1More details of the 23 recognized protected areas can be found here (full maps can be viewedhere).
National parks: areas reserved for nature and scenic views, protected for scientific, educational and entertainment purposes.
Protected landscapes: areas to be maintained as scenic spots for leisure and tourism.
The national parks and wildlife sanctuaries comply with category II and IV of IUCN’s classification system. The protected landscapes and multiple use areas conform to category V and VI of the IUCN’s classification system respectively.3
In 2008, the RGC adopted the Law on Protected Areas introducing and defining a new zoning system to manage the protected areas in Cambodia.6Chapter III of the law underlines the expansion and modification of protected areas categories which now include Ramsar sites, biosphere reserves, natural heritage sites and marine parks. This law is under the jurisdiction of the Ministry of Environment.7
