The box, or flat palm, is a traditional cut that is mostly found in the cheaper end of the market today. A single piece of latex for the palm and fingers is stitched to the back of the glove with inserts.
This means that the glove fits tighter on the hand and offers a little more grip, although it is more likely to show wear than a box cut glove. Roll finger cut
For example, a glove may have a roll cut on the index and fifth fingers of the glove to increase latex contact for catching, but a negative cut on the inner fingers to increase overall comfort and flexibility.
Like shoes, gloves come in a variety of sizes, usually between 4 and 12.
The material of the palm plays a big role in the performance of the gloves. Professional players prefer latex for more grip, but this is not the most hardy material and will degrade over time.
Palm thickness also plays a role, with thinner palms providing better touch on the ball, but less protection and cushioning.
These are a good option if you've had an injury in the past, but they don't protect against more common injuries, such as stump fingers or people stepping on your hand.
For this reason, we recommend avoiding this type of glove unless you have an existing injury.
