To make sure you buy the right pair of football gloves, there are a few things to keep in mind.
The first thing to consider when buying gloves for football is your position on the field.
From the outside, football gloves may all look the same, but in reality they are not.
Of course, if your position requires you to catch and protect the ball, then your gloves should have a different set of functions than when you're mainly dealing with blocking or tackling.
Their gloves should therefore be light, have as much grip as possible and should not impede the freedom of movement. This gives players more control over the ball.
They are designed light and smooth with extra grip around the fingers and thumbs, and some padding on the tops of the fingers for protection.
They are available in a wide range of colors and designs. After all, when you're in the spotlight, you also want to look your best!
The thin material provides better ball control and reduces heat development. The receiver-skill position gloves are also less expensive than gloves for linemen.
Positions that have little to do with the ball need more protection to prevent injuries. They need gloves with more padding.
These gloves often tend to be heavier and bulkier than the gloves used by skill players.
They also have finger reinforcements and the gloves – unlike receiver skill gloves – are not sticky on the palms, because grip is generally not necessary for these players.
The main purpose of these gloves is to protect. However, the linemen gloves are often more expensive than those for receivers/skill positions because you pay for the level of protection.
The hybrid gloves have both grip and padding, so that the players can handle the ball well, but at the same time will remain sufficiently protected.
The price of this type of gloves is in between that of the receivers/skilled position and linemen gloves.
Even in the rain, the gloves must remain functional, and therefore lose as little grip as possible. So keep that in mind when choosing your next pair of gloves.
The grip area on the surface of the gloves is considered a separate part of the main material.
To give gloves the right 'stickiness', silicone rubber is used.
The gloves of these brands are known for their stickiness and are very light in weight.
As mentioned before, this is the most important feature of gloves for linemen.
Do you want to protect your arms during the game in addition to your hands, check out my review American Football arm protection
The material of the gloves must be tear and abrasion resistant to ensure that they remain intact and continue to protect the player during the game.
In addition, gloves should not – or should lose as little as possible – their stickiness (for skill player gloves) or their stiffness (for linemen gloves).
Due to the texture and tight fit of gloves, your palms tend to sweat and become clammy if you wear them for extended periods of time.
Filling Go for a glove that has a flexible padding on the fingers and the top of the hand.
The gloves should feel soft on the skin and should not be too thick; you have to be able to maintain the 'feeling'.
For skill players it is important that the gloves are not too thick. This will negatively affect the handling of the ball.
Gloves should not require much maintenance or care. A good pair of gloves should be machine washable and quick to dry.
By the way, it is normal for the surface of skill player gloves to lose its tack over time. Then the gloves need to be replaced.
If the gloves are too small, they can affect your circulation and slow down your reaction time.
Please note that football gloves often come in slightly smaller sizes, so it is advisable to go for a size that is slightly larger than usual.
'Look good, play good' as they say. Do you go for a unique pair of gloves with bright colors or do you prefer to keep it basic?
