The secret is in the correct placement of the hands and fingers, the movement of the body, and the continuation of the arm movement even after you release the ball. You throw the perfect spiral by making a powerful and controlled movement.
2 How do I get the perfect spiral?
Remember: It takes time to learn how to throw a football, so don't get discouraged if you flop the first time. It is a process of trial and error.
Before you can even throw a ball, you need to know how to place your hands.
Bring your index finger near or directly to the tip of the ball.
Grab the ball with your fingers. Bend your fingers so that your knuckles lift slightly from the ball.
Your index finger should form an almost right triangle with your thumb. Use your fingers and the laces to get grip and control over the ball.
It also depends on the size of your hand. For example, someone with a smaller hand will not be able to grab the ball in the same way as someone with a larger hand.
To glove or not to glove?
Make sure your shoulders are aligned – and perpendicular – to the target. Your non-throwing shoulder faces the target.
Hold the ball with both hands, with the fingers of your dominant hand on the laces.
Bring the ball, which should be pointing up, behind your head, still with the laces on top.
Throw the ball forward past your head and release it at the highest point of your arm movement.
Finally, follow the movement forward with your back leg.
At the same time, you should start the throwing motion of your upper body.
Your back leg should follow your body forward so that you end up with both legs in an equal position parallel to each other.
Long, deep passes, on the other hand, are usually released further back behind the head to make an arc and get the distance needed.
It wouldn't make sense to stop the golf club movement by the ball. You want to get the full swing, and get the full momentum.
Instead, do a full swing. When you release the ball, make sure to flick your wrist down.
However, it is important to understand that no matter how many times you practice, not every throw will be perfect. Learning how to throw a spiral takes time.
A spiral – where the ball spins in perfect shape – ensures that the ball cuts through the wind and arrives at its destination as quickly and accurately as possible.
Holding the ball in a certain way allows you to manipulate it in just the right way so that when released, the result is predictable.
This means that it is easier for the receiver to predict where the ball will land and know exactly where to run to catch the ball.
Doing the exercise on one knee allows you to better focus on your grip, body position and release of the ball.
Because this exercise is all about technique, not throwing distance or throwing speed, the players can be placed close together, about 10 to 15 meters apart.
You can also try out different grabs and release techniques so that you understand what feels right for you.
Tip: Move your upper body back and forth as you throw the ball to mimic the movement you will experience during the game.
If you want to learn how to throw a football farther, perfecting your technique is the best place to start.
By consistently using the same technique, you'll build the torso and arm strength you need to throw at a greater distance.
And even though you may be limited in your movements during a match, you should always try to 'step' into a throw (ie take a step with the foot opposite to your throwing arm).
If you mainly want to build the distance of your throw, focus on practicing 'fly' routes.
