After you purchase the helmet, there are a few things you need to do to make sure it fits perfectly.
Put the helmet on your head
The chinstrap should be centered under the athlete's chin and snug. To make sure it's secure, open your mouth wide as if you're about to yawn.
Helmets with a four-point chin strap system require all four straps to be clipped in and tightened. Always follow the manufacturer's mounting instructions.
Two different types of padding can be used to fill the inside of the helmet shell. Helmet padding is either preformed or inflatable.
Put the helmet on your head and have someone insert the needle into the holes on the outside of the helmet.
If you feel that the jaw pads are too tight or too loose and you cannot inflate them, change them.
Please note that you will fit the helmet with the hairstyle you will be wearing during training and competitions. The fit of a helmet can change if the athlete's hairstyle changes.
Also check that the ear holes are aligned with your ears and that the insert on the front of the helmet covers your head from the center of the forehead to the back of the head.
Now move your head from left to right and from top to bottom. When the helmet fits properly, there should be no shifting of the forehead or skin against the pads.
If all this is not possible, then a smaller helmet may be desirable.
If the helmet can be removed with the chinstrap attached, the fit is too loose and will need to be adjusted.
Keep your helmet clean, both inside and out, with warm water and possibly a mild detergent (no strong detergents). Never soak your helmet or loose parts.
Do not place your helmet near heat sources. Also, never let anyone sit on your helmet.
Reconditioning (reconditioning) Reconditioning involves an expert inspecting and restoring a used helmet by: repairing cracks or damage, replacing missing parts, testing for safety and recertifying for use.
You should never try to repair your helmet yourself. Also, never use a helmet that is cracked or broken, or that has broken parts or filling.
Before the season and every now and then during the season, check that your helmet is still intact and that nothing is missing.
More information Like many squash fans, you may have wondered before, is squash an Olympic sport?
Squash is not an Olympic sport and has never been in the history of the Olympics.
There are many things to know about the history of the WSF's attempts to squash Olympic status, and I'll take a look at these, as well as the possible reasons why it still hasn't been included in the Olympics.
However, anger and frustration will only get you so far in life. At some point, there has to be a certain amount of introspection.
There needs to be a firmer understanding of what the IOC is trying to achieve under the leadership of Thomas Bach, the current president of the sports board.
Furthermore, Bach is a lawyer by profession and a reformer. That's something more important to note than his screen background.
However, both players are also shadows of the players they once were and both are in their thirties.
The organizers of Paris 2024 have always made it clear that they want to include sports that appeal to young people in France.
There is a commercial aspect, which we briefly covered earlier in this segment,
The World Squash Federation has always been keen that the sport's governing body has taken huge strides in capturing young people's imaginations that squash is innovative.
However, the reality is that squash faces very stiff competition from hipper sports, most of which are not traditional sports like squash, which have captured the imagination of young people over the past two decades.
As most people know by now, squash has already been beaten by breakdance before Paris 2024.
Like it or not, this is where the world is going. Breaking, already seen during the 2018 Youth Olympics in Buenos Aires, was particularly popular and most would say very successful.
I can say with great certainty that your chances of finding a break dancer are far greater than those of a squash enthusiast or even a squash court.
The youth of Paris is more culturally diverse than most societies in the western world.
An Olympic medal is the sport's highest honor. Every elite player agrees that the Olympics would take the sport to another level and the Olympic champion of Squash is a title that every player wants.
Squash features world-class athletes from countries that traditionally do not produce Olympians. Including squash in the Olympics will raise awareness of the Olympic movement in these countries, and will also promote better funding for the development of the sport.
