While quarterbacks often choose to wear rib guards, skill players (such as wide receivers and running backs) often wear the more stylish back plate.
The quality of a back plate depends on its material, construction process, durability and effectiveness in fulfilling its function.
Protection comes first, of course, but style is also important and perhaps price. It is crucial that you get a back plate that is well put together and that will last all season.
Before I present you the best back plates, I want to give you a sneak peek of my favorite model: the Battle Sports Back Plate. The Battle Sports back plate is selling very well.
1.6 Strength and durability
3.3 How do I attach my back plate to my shoulder pads?
A back plate, also called a 'back flap', is an extra protection for the lower back, which is attached to the back of the body the shoulder pads will be confirmed.
Below you will find an explanation about the factors that you should take into account when purchasing your next back plate.
The back plates can protect your lower back, spine and kidneys from any trauma that could have been very dangerous in other cases.
Wide receivers are most at risk of being hit in the lower back. Whenever they catch a ball, they expose their lower back and spine to the defender.
Players can choose to wear a back plate if they see fit.
Another example is the black stickers under the eyes with letters and/or numbers - worn more for 'swag' than to keep the sun or light out of the eyes.
The style where players let the back plate hang out from under the jersey has become illegal in most competitions.
They may even send a player off the field of play until he has tucked his shirt in.
Consider the size and shape of the desired back plate.
The larger the back plate, the more your lower back is covered and the better it is protected. Make sure that the back plate offers sufficient protection to your lower back and kidneys.
The weight of the back plate has a direct impact on your performance on the pitch.
A heavier back plate will make your game much more difficult because you will move slower and have trouble turning.
Back plates are usually made with EVA foam for shock absorption and have very simple designs. In principle, the thicker the foam, the better the shock absorption.
If you want to lose as little speed as possible, you will have to go for a lighter back plate and (unfortunately) have to sacrifice some protection.
The stronger and more durable, the better protected you will be. You need a really strong one that can protect you from the ill effects of collisions, tackles and falls.
