Baseball is a wonderful sport that, strangely enough, is still very much undervalued in the Netherlands. But it has been gaining traction for a number of years, which is why it is also good to think about the referees of this beautiful ball sport: the Umpire.
We can divide the clothing into two categories: the right shoes and the right outfit.
Because you play on a field and still move around quite a bit, it is also recommended for a baseball player to wear shoes with studs, specifically made for the sandy ground of the baseball field.
However, the referee usually wear sports shoes like this one to be agile and yet not be too fixed. They have to lead the game and are not an active part, as is the case with football, for example.
(View more clothing items)
To make the game of baseball as fair as possible, there are usually umpires on the field to call the rules. Sometimes the umpires are referred to as “Blue” or “Ump” for short.
Most games have at least two umpires, so you can be behind the plate and one in the field. In Major League Baseball there are four umpires.
The plate umpire, or plate umpire, is behind home plate and is responsible for calling balls and strikes. This umpire also calls about batter, fair and foul balls at third and first base and plays at home plate.
Base umpires are usually assigned to a base. In the major leagues, there are three base referees, one for each base.
In many youth leagues there is only one basic referee. This umpire must cross the field to attempt to make the call.
Here are some of the common signs referees will see:
Yelling at the referee or loudly calling out contentious will never help your cause and is not good sportsmanship.
This square is called the baseball diamond. The bases are called home plate (this is where the batter is), first base, second base and third base.
In a standard baseball field, the distance between each base is 90 feet. The distance from the pitchers mound to home plate is 60 feet 6 inches.
A baseball game is defined by outs and innings. A game usually consists of 9 innings, but can contain fewer innings at many levels of play.
On getting a third out, the inning is over or it's the opposing team's turn. The winner of the baseball game is the team with the most runs at the end of the last inning.
A strike is when the baseball is thrown over the home plate area, above the batter's knees and below the batter's belt.
A strike is also called when a batter fouls the ball. A foul ball only counts as a first or second stroke.
If the pitcher throws 4 balls, the batter must advance to first base. This is called a walk.
The first goal is to catch the baseball before it hits the ground. If the fielders do this, the batter is out and all other base runners must return to their original base before being tagged or they are out.
A force out is when the base runner has nowhere to go but to the next base.
To tag a runner, the defending player must tag the runner with the baseball or with the glove holding the baseball.
In this case, they should label the runner.
2 Should you serve from the left or right side in squash?
2.2 What are you aiming for with your service?
3 Do you get a second serve in squash?
Funnily enough, while serving isn't very important in squash, most field lines are actually for serving!
Can you hit the red line in squash?
The squash ball can touch part of a line, then it is already out. Similarly, your foot cannot touch the service line while serving, or it is a direct service foul.
This ensures that the ball is brought into play reasonably and it does not always lead to a point immediately. This is also why service is not very important in squash.
You can also choose again from which side you want to serve from the moment you win the service from your opponent. You then have to switch sides if you win consecutive points, which means you can't serve from the same side every time.
Your opponent wins the toss at the start of the match and chooses to serve on the right
You win the 3rd point and now get the chance to decide which service box you want to start from, left or right You choose the right side
Your opponent wins the next point, and gets to choose again from which side he wants to serve
Which side is best served from?
Since most players are right handed, I almost always start serving from the right side, on their backhand.
Now that you know the lines and rules on the squash court, we can work on where exactly to hit the ideal serve.
To do this, there are a few spots to aim for on the wall, as well as places to position your feet.
Place your foot in this corner of the service box when serving from the right side of the court. Your other foot will be outward toward the “T”.
Your right side service should contact the left wall here. Why?
They either have the option to hit the ball just before or just after the wall contact. This makes for difficult timing, and a more likely weak return!
Should the ball bounce when serving in squash?
When bouncing after the ball has hit the back wall, the ball must bounce into the opponent's box.
It is also easier to maintain precision, control and accuracy with private service. It's a delicate shot that's easier to control than an overhand serve.
Many people will have this question because underhand serve is the most common.
You can usually give this more speed, which can put some pressure on your opponent. However, most non-beginners will have no problem returning this service.
Furthermore, the squash ball also has a downward direction instead of an upward direction, which means that your opponent can bounce the ball first or hit it around the hip.
For these reasons, I would only recommend using overhand services as a surprising change from a more accurate underhand.
Instead, it will land towards the center of the field, giving your opponent a big advantage.
In short, it's a risky shot that I see mostly used by more experienced players, trying to win easily after a serve, but even then just as a change from the underhand serve to surprise.
The only professional I've seen use it is James Willstrop, and it doesn't seem like it gives him much of an advantage against experienced players as they can return that serve reasonably well too.
The backhand serve is the most common in squash, and should be used the most. Why?
Again, the higher the better, without crossing the out line.
See the ball lanes in the image below:
Because there must always be 1 foot in the service box, a right-handed player with a backhand serve from the correct box can direct the ball closer to the sidewall to his opponent.
