In most places where it is also played at a very competitive level it is gaining ground. What was once a sport only the wealthy could afford, squash is now more accessible to people of all income levels.
4 More countries where squash is growing
As the game of squash becomes more and more popular in the United States, they have added a number of new tournaments, including the biggest new tournament, the US Open Squash Doubles Tournament.
As competition grows, so does the need for more jobs and that is exactly what is happening in the US. New jobs are popping up across the country, encouraging new players to participate in the sport.
Since many juniors are so interested in squash, it's no secret that colleges have had to adapt to its growing popularity as well. Many Ivy League schools now offer financial aid packages to elite squash players, just like they do in other sports like basketball and play football.
Squash is becoming more and more popular in Egypt
With some of the world's best players hailing from Egypt, it's no wonder the sport of squash is thriving in that country.
In the current World Rankings, players from Egypt have two prominent places:
At most colleges and preparatory schools, younger students are exposed to the sport at a young age, giving them more time to practice and acquire technique and skills.
In the Women's Squash Association, Alison Waters and Laura Massero hold the number three and four places, respectively.
Although the United States, Egypt and England are three of the most thriving countries for the sport of squash, the popularity of the game is not limited to these countries.
Although these are the countries where the current top players come from, the game is played in 185 countries around the world.
With this growth, it is possible that squash will one day be as common as baseball and tennis and played recreationally among families around the world.
