Then there are the rules of the sport itself: how do you play the game, and what about the scoring?
For example, the ball must be served from the open hand and be at least 16 cm in the air for service. Then the player with the bat hits the ball via his own half of the table over the net on the opponent's half.
I will also explain a little better about the most important rule in table tennis; so the storage.
3 The most important storage rules
Each player or team has a half of the table. Both halves are separated by means of a net.
You do this in such a way that your opponent can no longer or can no longer return the ball correctly to your half of the table.
You get a point if your opponent serves the ball incorrectly or otherwise returns it incorrectly
Most matches are based on the 'best of five' principle, where you have to win three matches (out of five) to definitely win the match against your opponent.
And in the event that a deciding game is played, such as the fifth game of five games, then the half of the table is also changed.
The striker must hold or throw the ball straight up from the open, flat hand at least 16 cm. The player then hits the ball with the bat via his own half of the table over the net on the opponent's half.
In addition, you may not deprive your opponent of the view of the ball and he/she must therefore be able to see the service well. The ball may not touch the net.
With a good serve you can immediately get an advantage over your opponent:
That means a surcharge per person, at a time.
Read here by the way whether you can hold the table tennis bat with two hands (or not?)
If the service is good, the opponent must return the ball.
In this case, it can be done via the net.
In doubles, where two against two is played instead of one against one, the rules are a bit different.
The players then take turns. This means that you always return the ball from the same opponent.
When two servings have been made, the players of the team will switch places, so that at the next serve, the teammate becomes the server.
If the game is still undecided after 10 minutes, players take turns serving
Should the game be disrupted through no fault of the players, the point must be replayed.
In order not to make matches last too long, there is a rule in official matches that if a game doesn't have a winner after 10 minutes (unless both players have already scored at least 9 points), the players serve alternately.
Furthermore, players are required to play with a bat that has red rubber on one side and black rubber on the other.
Service “Out” Line: You must hit the ball above this line on your serve. Why?
Out line: This rule applies to services as well as all shots during the rallies. All shots on or above the line are out.
When a squash match starts, whoever wins the racket spin or coin toss decides whether to serve from the right or left side.
He/she wins the next 2 points and serves left first, then right again
As you will understand we want to make it as difficult as possible for our opponent to hit the ball with a good shot.
Similarly, you have your foot in corner 2 on a serve from the left side of the court.
This is where your opponent will most likely try to hit the ball, and it's high enough that your opponent has to reach above his power for a volley, which is harder than hitting at waist level. The higher the better, without hitting the top line of course!
Similarly, your left serve should make contact with the right wall here, making it as hard as possible for your opponent.
The ball does not have to bounce on a squash serve. You must first hit the ball against the back wall without touching the ground first, then your opponent may also return the ball without it having bounced.
This is the most common service in squash, and should be used the most. Why?
Usually, your opponent can bounce this serve, off the side and back wall, and you'll have a simple ball to return. Higher speed also means less accuracy in your service.
These are much easier returns than a high volley.
The lob serve is a variation of the underhand serve, in which the squash ball is hit high on the back wall with an upward line and makes quick contact with the side wall just below the outside line.
Also, performing the lobe service has a high chance of landing above the alignment on the sidewall.
It's also easier to control your precision and accuracy when serving from backhand.
The orange trajectory, from a right-handed backhand, is almost parallel to the sidewall, giving your opponent less room to make a return. There is a higher chance your opponent will scrape the sidewall with his racket and a higher chance of a weak service return.
Playing with your forehand from a correct position in the box means that the squash ball hits the sidewall at a greater angle, giving your opponent a lot more room to hit the ball.
To make the best serve return in squash:
Place yourself at least 1 racket + an arm's length away from the side wall, to give yourself room to hit the ball
Attack a weak serve with a straight drop or other offensive shot. A good serve will force you to play a straight length or a crosscourt if you have the space.
For a left-handed player, hit a backhand serve from the left box and forehand serve from the right.
Move one foot out of the service area toward the “T”. Keep the other foot in the box for your serve.
Vary your underhand serve every now and then with an overhand or lob serve. This is optional and is only meant to surprise your opponent
